<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasrid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1232–1492</span> · Also known as Banu Nasr, Banu al-Ahmar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nasrid dynasty was the last Muslim ruling house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, founded by Muhammad I ibn Nasr, who rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjona">Arjona</a> in 1232 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1238. From Granada it ruled a mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> reaching to Málaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, often as a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Its rulers built most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> palaces, and the dynasty ended when Muhammad XII surrendered Granada to Ferdinand and Isabella in 1492.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Nasrid dynasty" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Nasrid_Granada_(modern_emblazonment).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
